WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.250
Somewhere on the skin, a drop of sweat rises

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.400
ENTER THE VILLAIN: SWEAT POOL · SKILL: POOLING UNDER SEALED PATCHES

3
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.400
HANJI HERO · ORIGIN OF THE BREATHABLE PATCH

4
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.900
WHAT IS A MICRONEEDLE? A tiny needle that passes the outer skin layer (typically 0.15–1 mm long) (Conventional microneedle patch, sealed polymer backing)

5
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.900
When sweat has no way out, it pools under the patch (Illustration of problems reported in earlier studies · not measured in this paper)

6
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.400
THE PROBLEM: Sweat pools under impermeable polymer backings, so drug delivery and sensing were hard to combine

7
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.900
Same water-drop test. Different ending? (5 μL DI water · CONTROL: sealed PMMA plastic | THIS WORK: porous hanji (MP3))

8
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.000
CONTROL: Sealed surface, nowhere to soak in | THIS WORK: Linked pores between fibers pull water in · contact angle 72.19° → 7.96° · 5 μL DI-water droplet (Illustration · 5 μL DI-water droplet test · time compressed, in-between steps illustrative · numbers are hanji values (PMMA reported only as little change))

9
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.000
CONTROL: STAYS ON TOP | THIS WORK: ABSORBED WITHIN 6 s

10
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.000
Interconnected pores between fibers draw water in and spread it sideways (water droplet test · less sweat pooling is expected but still needs tests under continuous sweating)

11
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.500
Water droplet: Reference (PMMA, prior work) Barely changes (PMMA control) | This work (hanji patch) Absorbed within 6 s · contact angle 72.19° → 7.96° · 5 μL DI-water droplet

12
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.500
Resin–backing peel stress: Reference (PMMA, prior work) 10.85 N/mm² (PMMA control) | This work (hanji patch) 17.89 N/mm² · peel test of a cured resin block on the backing

13
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.500
One patch, two jobs: Reference (PMMA, prior work) Prior work (introduction): rarely combined in one breathable device | This work (hanji patch) Needles + uric acid sensor · 3D-printed microneedles + screen-printed electrode

14
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.400
Uric acid sensor: 9.3 nM detection limit (in PBS buffer, by DPV · linear in three ranges: 0.01–0.1, 0.1–0.8 and 0.8–8 μM) · uric acid: a marker linked to gout

15
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.000
3D-PRINTED MICRONEEDLES: 954 μm, mean insertion depth 954.32 ± 5.98 μm (excised rat skin (ex vivo))

16
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.400
DRUG LOADING: N1 · plain cone 12.6% | N4 · side-trimmed 25.36% (tested with rhodamine B, a fluorescent model compound, not a drug)

17
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.000
WHERE IT COULD GO · FUTURE CONCEPT (CONCEPT SKETCH): Drug delivery and sweat uric acid sensing on one patch · Testing them together comes next (So far each was tested separately · drug release: model compound, rat skin · uric acid: buffer and sweat samples · not yet worn or clinically tested)

18
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.800
Breathable hanji patch: microneedles + sweat sensor

19
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:35.700
...still practicing that landing.
